# Copy to .env. Read by the Makefile's docker-* targets and, for anything
# compose substitutes, by docker compose itself. Plain KEY=value, no quotes.

# Which compose overlay the docker-* targets use with docker-compose.yaml:
#   macos — Docker Desktop on macOS or Windows; mounts the herdr socket as a
#           file, which is the only form that can connect() across the VM.
#   linux — native Docker Engine; mounts ~/.config/herdr as a directory,
#           which keeps working after herdr restarts (a file mount there
#           pins the inode and goes dead).
# Unset, the Makefile picks from `uname -s` (Darwin -> macos, Linux -> linux),
# so the common case needs no .env at all. `make docker-up PLATFORM=linux`
# beats this file.
#PLATFORM=macos

# The published port is also the origin the browser sends, and the image's
# CMD hard-codes --allowed-origin http://127.0.0.1:5173 and
# http://localhost:5173. Publishing on a different host port means editing
# the `ports:` entry in docker-compose.yaml *and* overriding those origins
# with a `command:` — otherwise every /ws handshake is refused with 403 and
# the reason appears only in `docker compose logs bridge`.
